# Public client identifiers only: every EXPO_PUBLIC_ value is built into the app, so nothing secret
# goes here. Copy this file to .env (never committed) and fill it in. Paste each value right after
# the "=", with no quotes or spaces.

# ============================== Supabase (supabase.com/dashboard) ==============================
# Only these two values come from Supabase. Ignore the "Legacy anon, service_role API keys" tab and
# the "Legacy JWT secret": they are deprecated and nothing in RoadWise uses them. Never put a secret
# key (sb_secret_...) or the database password here; the edge functions get their keys from
# Supabase automatically, and `npx supabase db push` asks for the password itself.
# For the local stack instead, `npx supabase status` prints both (API_URL and PUBLISHABLE_KEY).

# Project URL, shaped https://<project-ref>.supabase.co.
#   Open the project > click "Connect" (top bar) > "Mobile" tab > "Expo React Native" > copy the URL.
#   Or build it: the ref is the code after /project/ in the dashboard address, also shown at
#   Project Settings > General > "Project ID".
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# Publishable key, starts with sb_publishable_.
#   Open the project > Project Settings (gear, bottom left) > "API Keys" > tab "Publishable and
#   secret API keys" > "Publishable key" section > copy the "default" key.
#   The same key is in the "Connect" dialog above. If the tab shows no publishable key, click
#   "Create new API keys" there first.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ================== Google Cloud Console (console.cloud.google.com), not Supabase ==================
# Pick the RoadWise project in the project picker (top left). If Google asks, set up the consent
# screen first: Google Auth Platform > "Branding" and "Audience" (add your Google account as a test
# user while the app is in Testing).
# The three client ids: Google Auth Platform > "Clients" > "+ Create client" (the same clients are
# also listed under APIs & Services > Credentials). Each id ends in .apps.googleusercontent.com;
# copy the "Client ID", never the client secret.

# Application type "Web application". No origins or redirect URIs needed.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# Application type "iOS", Bundle ID com.lurp.safedrive.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=

# Application type "Android", Package name com.lurp.safedrive, plus the SHA-1 of the key that signs
# the build: run `npx -y eas-cli@24.7.0 credentials` > Android > the build profile > copy "SHA1
# Fingerprint". (A Play Store install is re-signed by Google: its SHA-1 is in Play Console > Test
# and release > App integrity > App signing; that needs a second Android client.) Then open the new
# client > "Advanced settings" > turn on "Enable custom URI scheme" (the app returns to
# com.lurp.safedrive:/oauthredirect).
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=

# Maps SDK for Android key, starts with AIza. Billing must be on for the project.
#   APIs & Services > Library > "Maps SDK for Android" > Enable.
#   APIs & Services > Credentials > "+ Create credentials" > "API key" > copy it, then edit the key:
#   Application restrictions "Android apps" > add package com.lurp.safedrive with the same SHA-1 as
#   above; API restrictions "Restrict key" > "Maps SDK for Android" > Save.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_ANDROID_KEY=

# ================== Read by the Supabase CLI only (never by the app) ==================
# `npx supabase config push` reads this for the production Google sign-in provider: the three
# Google client ids above joined with commas, no spaces, web first:
#   <web id>,<ios id>,<android id>
SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# `npx supabase config push` also reads these two for the production confirmation mail (the
# 6-digit code an email sign-up types into the app), sent through Resend's SMTP. Both must be set
# before every push: an unset sender goes out as the literal text "env(AUTH_SMTP_SENDER)", and an
# unset key sends no password.
# The From address of the mail, on the domain verified in Resend, such as no-reply@<your domain>.
AUTH_SMTP_SENDER=
# The Resend API key (starts with re_): resend.com > API Keys > "Create API key", permission
# "Sending access", limited to the sending domain. It is a secret, so it does not go in this file:
# put RESEND_API_KEY=re_... in supabase/.env (never committed; the CLI reads it too) or set it in
# the shell that runs the push. Listed here only so the name is written down:
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# ============ Edge Function secrets (Supabase, never in this file and never in the app) ============
# The Live Activity's push-to-start (live-activity-start) signs its APNs requests with an APNs auth
# key. These four are Supabase secrets, set once on the linked project, not values for .env:
#   Apple Developer > Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles > Keys > "+" > enable "Apple Push
#   Notifications service (APNs)" > download AuthKey_<KEY ID>.p8 (it downloads once; keep it private).
#   The Team ID is under Membership details.
#
#   npx supabase secrets set APNS_KEY_ID=<10-character key id> APNS_TEAM_ID=<10-character team id> \
#     APNS_BUNDLE_ID=com.lurp.safedrive APNS_PRIVATE_KEY="$(cat AuthKey_<KEY ID>.p8)"
#
# Without them live-activity-start answers `unconfigured` and the phone's own "Recording your drive"
# notification stands in. Listed here only so the names are written down somewhere:
# APNS_KEY_ID=
# APNS_TEAM_ID=
# APNS_BUNDLE_ID=
# APNS_PRIVATE_KEY=
